
# WHY THIS FILE IS CALLED "secrets-common" AND NOT JUST "secrets".
#
# Kamal reads exactly two files: this one, and ".kamal/secrets.<name>" when you
# deploy to a named machine with `-d <name>`. A plain ".kamal/secrets" is read
# ONLY when there is no `-d`. So while this file was called that, every
# `kamal deploy -d pi2` failed with "Secret 'KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD' not found"
# — it was not looking here at all.
#
# "-common" means "read for every deploy, named or not". One file, used by both,
# so the two can never drift apart. Only put something in a ".kamal/secrets.<name>"
# file if that machine genuinely needs a DIFFERENT value.

# Secrets defined here are available for reference under registry/password, env/secret, builder/secrets,
# and accessories/*/env/secret in config/deploy.yml. All secrets should be pulled from either
# password manager, ENV, or a file. DO NOT ENTER RAW CREDENTIALS HERE! This file needs to be safe for git.

# WS0.3: this file is NOT executed as a shell script. Kamal parses it with
# Dotenv.parse + InlineCommandSubstitution, which evaluates `KEY=$(...)`
# assignments but silently skips bare statements — so a `set -euo pipefail`
# here never ran and provided no fail-closed guard (the F-iter1-006 comment
# documented semantics the file did not actually have). What protects the
# deploy is Kamal itself: `kamal secrets fetch`/`extract` run in-process and
# RAISE on failure (kamal >= 2.6), aborting before any empty value (e.g. the
# F-iter1-006 ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="" boot) can propagate. For an explicit
# pre-flight, add an executable .kamal/hooks/pre-connect hook — hooks run as a
# real shell and abort the deploy on non-zero exit.

# Option 1: Read secrets from the environment
# The registry password for SWAY_REGISTRY. config/deploy.yml signs in as
# "anonymous"; change that and supply a real credential here if your registry
# requires one. A registry without authentication is only safe on a network you
# trust. Kamal requires the field to be non-empty either way.
#
# It still uses GITHUB_ACCESS_TOKEN whenever that is set, which is what
# scripts/deploy.sh provides out of .env.kamal, so a normal deploy is unchanged.
# The fallback is the point: .env.kamal is loaded by that script and by nothing
# else, so every bare `kamal` command died at the sign-in step with
#
#     flag needs an argument: 'p' in -p        (docker exit status 125)
#
# and moving to a new host runs entirely on bare commands — app stop,
# accessory stop, proxy boot, app boot.
# @lat: [[move-to-a-new-host]]
#
# `printenv` rather than the shell's own ${VAR:-unused}: this file is read by
# dotenv, which replaces $VAR itself before any shell sees the line, so the
# default never survives. Measured — ${GITHUB_ACCESS_TOKEN:-unused} came back as
# the string ":-unused}". Leaving nothing for dotenv to replace is what works.
KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=$(printenv GITHUB_ACCESS_TOKEN || echo unused)

# Option 2: Read secrets via a command
# RAILS_MASTER_KEY=$(cat config/master.key)

# Option 3: Read secrets via kamal secrets helpers
# These will handle logging in and fetching the secrets in as few calls as possible
# There are adapters for 1Password, LastPass + Bitwarden
#
# SECRETS=$(kamal secrets fetch --adapter 1password --account my-account --from MyVault/MyItem KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD RAILS_MASTER_KEY)
# KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=$(kamal secrets extract KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD $SECRETS)
# RAILS_MASTER_KEY=$(kamal secrets extract RAILS_MASTER_KEY $SECRETS)

